} :question/id #uuid "bff2c616-f045-5186-8916-8acfc055fce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333304" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/36/36ae671944545eaae9027ef0d4a876e12ceef17d01be0ad881d32dfdcb8ad963.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | $1.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | $4.95$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd431b8daf3ad9c1b25225af9d64b1e4876e4e1a90e1dfd010de8ed134ae323c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354f21b2-96ab-5d34-8629-17b1cd8d23d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551791} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee3cf3b-86e3-5e57-83f5-b5451cd4c7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551893} {:answer/id #uuid "800e78fd-bc77-5f21-8b03-8b51fc29c534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551970} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ccdf0e-7384-5c78-bdab-6715b3b614a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552133} {:answer/id #uuid "f270c863-ecf9-5d37-bac9-47ebceb975b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551791} :answer-field/id #uuid "3231d9e1-5db0-532b-9d06-68eafedeb402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "226b9b94-926b-577f-834a-35ff659d1159" :question/math-academy-id "q-102338" :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $15{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $$\n\\displaystyle [x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 4+(-15)\\cdot 3}{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-45}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n15{x}_{2}=15\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a31093c-3bc1-5604-8b68-60bb29456071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551690} {:answer/id #uuid "1c236ae5-4aa5-5314-b3d7-d9f14364f42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}62 \\\\ 45\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551725} {:answer/id #uuid "55bb8ae9-83ab-5bd6-b3fa-7323cb3b3ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}62 & 45\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551870} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b3471b-58e2-538a-96d3-b94d24b16af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552101} {:answer/id #uuid "f9195545-d1e3-513f-91e6-f5228e90b375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551725} :answer-field/id #uuid "560cd508-cebe-5335-8021-8a9e70cdc673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47594296-da7d-5903-b008-c5f5265a8817" :question/math-academy-id "q-269489" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $$\n\\displaystyle L(x)={(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)\n$$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)=\\frac{1}{2}A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b) \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 34\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}62 \\\\ 45\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30da5234-4042-5fab-bc17-28a88bfb9de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-26 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551781} {:answer/id #uuid "37e4fbe8-c0f7-58ed-9b71-06218e634007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}28 & -16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551799} {:answer/id #uuid "868cbcd3-651b-5e03-a865-b0dfaf92d893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551989} {:answer/id #uuid "d0501560-e02a-5299-a647-3098cd11d592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}52 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552158} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c321cc-baeb-5602-885c-ed6669f5d604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552158} :answer-field/id #uuid "6730a75f-f272-5493-a8bf-085f7b4def46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9669b6bb-a27d-584f-935a-b35adacb11c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-269476" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}52 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f2c097-7bd7-557a-a116-a684e091c780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553049} {:answer/id #uuid "591aca34-5191-5fe1-9db7-320758bc5e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553061} {:answer/id #uuid "a85a19cd-59c2-5d28-9c89-e1a6319ddd48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553206} {:answer/id #uuid "c4582499-f706-5cc9-8e35-eb95cf03cc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553258} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d4bacd-b2da-531e-8234-b3604fea9dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553319}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553049} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfd3e00c-418a-520a-80fa-a7b949ac974e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a810631-1bae-5445-bc88-4aea289acecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335078" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bfe2ae9-aec8-50cf-849c-115d91f5165f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552950} {:answer/id #uuid "786ae3e8-66e4-5326-b092-7941c56f8c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553113} {:answer/id #uuid "82a270b6-94b8-5f5f-8060-a6d5207f6169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553130} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6f2836-2dba-56ff-9d23-d8bc722d1e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553336} {:answer/id #uuid "fae32f0d-4f34-5b6e-8153-95de23dd818e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553336} :answer-field/id #uuid "9249f96b-64b8-59e4-9bf4-1d116673b015" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b02e6de2-bb8c-5f1a-89b9-7e259d335937" :question/math-academy-id "q-335195" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e6be835-40ec-54ab-80f9-e88f6e1c2e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553900} {:answer/id #uuid "58d46ea7-049e-5a25-a6c2-5ad53ae4d659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554043} {:answer/id #uuid "a99fb21f-5f60-57d9-893f-48f42ff18616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554246} {:answer/id #uuid "d7117175-fba5-5e21-8e5f-4b0885591d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554337} {:answer/id #uuid "fd679fa5-f8b4-58d0-86cf-0a7829e9b639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554043} :answer-field/id #uuid "d228661a-466c-50ca-9003-6059a883594a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "555dd099-33d6-5089-b5ce-f831f111dabe" :question/math-academy-id "q-95363" :question/problem "$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{12}+{p}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}\\{a\\}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{12}+{p}_{33}=1+2=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21856be1-b1fa-5f91-8a39-4aebb5209096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553926} {:answer/id #uuid "8a8f88fa-1943-5b66-9a8b-582f194c3e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554163} {:answer/id #uuid "9c971210-23ba-5a3d-9d48-b48f9a6fc7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554213} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c1e9fb-4917-514f-8d69-f634fa62d636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554289} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a9c01f-6c85-52fc-b668-f80c2e6a714d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554402}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554402} :answer-field/id #uuid "79a8ed53-1004-51ec-854e-1bc8377232da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af173c80-5734-52f5-887a-4f2d2e37a60e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37159" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259c4561-f4f6-5e22-b3d1-59bfa17cceb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555076} {:answer/id #uuid "28648a1d-d4da-54ce-9c6d-02600da5d23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555086} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0ab33a-2f17-5383-81d5-4116be86b3ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555322} {:answer/id #uuid "93ebf0c4-eb4c-55d4-8425-5fd308be1a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555350} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bdbc8e-eb7b-512b-8a68-5266113be851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555435}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555435} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ba6166-7a5e-5458-bda8-6a3496ebc681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "382e043a-e5b7-50fb-a8b9-9a5ba4e6f38b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94869" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064be7dc-c00e-551d-b59c-9b5c0cc3eb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555009} {:answer/id #uuid "79fc7d50-2157-579c-9285-9ef0a16c6060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555283} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7e68b0-ecab-5c63-a1f5-236ebb590064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555412} {:answer/id #uuid "bd519e66-5074-5ce2-a475-b9652a52c6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555449} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4bed5-b3f8-50de-9ea2-abc0ad3054b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555449} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f65add-d1f7-57d9-bd73-9ef3d190de02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab0273b9-503a-530e-8fea-6b6048661bee" :question/math-academy-id "q-107535" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd98a66-67bb-5fc9-b62b-aeb298847393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556494} {:answer/id #uuid "76a7ec27-93f5-544c-9463-ed42ad6cb9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556698} {:answer/id #uuid "87c293eb-dc8e-51ca-a5f1-bbafb0c786eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556750} {:answer/id #uuid "b16396cd-321a-5d69-abaf-cebcedad9ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556865} {:answer/id #uuid "d04a8b92-03b1-5f6f-a205-b58ee49a644d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556865} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2882232-ab0e-5766-a29a-3d2353385bb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "196a5704-c0f5-5a90-b92b-f7fa820f51b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40131" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1274a3e1-55b9-552d-b003-12310c1f94da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556469} {:answer/id #uuid "420e7c72-67c3-54bb-8ebc-2aa8890493a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556577} {:answer/id #uuid "65290f11-7840-570f-a534-0fa4fca0ae31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556664} {:answer/id #uuid "7f46a36f-9fe5-5805-871e-6936924e326a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556722} {:answer/id #uuid "c548e49e-a7cd-5be9-b97c-4c57df80a6e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556664} :answer-field/id #uuid "9220e030-d050-59e1-aeee-1e7f3bb39fe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9735b81a-0c65-5842-9564-1b88b92db55e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32723" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $M=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $M$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $M$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}M=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}M & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}M & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ M & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ M & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f0d8e4c-11e0-585b-98a8-e1e65e946a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556531} {:answer/id #uuid "360d0293-cec9-58b0-9fa0-f3f513479858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556547} {:answer/id #uuid "840aef6c-7174-5636-bec4-0b4c79dba19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556740} {:answer/id #uuid "c713eb9d-b36e-5847-8500-ce5007937f65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556908} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c1dda6-8000-59cb-8a0d-65f0284741cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556547} :answer-field/id #uuid "76fb5c7d-850b-5730-b4f9-b378b453ae17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e07c46c7-d38b-5617-a5f3-42fc8a91e6b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75098" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011655a2-c520-5cd0-a439-defc348c17b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558974} {:answer/id #uuid "48815a36-e11f-5e50-85f2-1df30f980d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558980} {:answer/id #uuid "91e08695-6405-567c-8d4e-0b8f3347a5f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558986} {:answer/id #uuid "a0bde4ca-48f5-5c86-ad8e-88083b8a8731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558987} {:answer/id #uuid "ed23ef92-0aad-5b95-947e-ba7002f159c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u \\sqrt{v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558987} :answer-field/id #uuid "32a31c4b-f831-5da9-8f29-1b931744a7c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ce03692-58d4-5830-8d97-d77704c638fb" :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\quad D=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=\\langle u + v,\\,3 - v,\\,3 u + 5 v + 1\\rangle$ for $0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(3 u + 5 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,3\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(3 u + 5 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,-1,\\,5\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v &=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  &=\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 3,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\| &=\\left\\|\\langle 3,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\right\\| \\\\  &=\\sqrt{\\left(3\\right)^2+\\left(-2\\right)^2+\\left(-1\\right)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{9+4+1} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S &=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  &=\\iint_D \\sqrt{14}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b115ea7-8c75-582e-bcb9-c63407864ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u \\sqrt{16 v + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558975} {:answer/id #uuid "3bbc89bf-4b41-500d-a74f-a5a23cc77a9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{4 u^{2} + v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558978} {:answer/id #uuid "43feaff8-ab49-5cc0-a22e-c79061a43457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 v \\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558979} {:answer/id #uuid "733b93c8-4090-5697-9f6e-1b3b25a0447f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{- 4 u^{2} + v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558984} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f43bf4-274b-5f91-890a-212b06e6e8c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} \\sqrt{16 u^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558979} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cf6d408-766c-507b-a6fd-42b33e06fe2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c17b6edc-1f0e-51cb-a0ce-11f93aba061f" :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\quad D=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=\\langle 2 u^{2},\\,u - v^{2},\\,v^{2}\\rangle$ for $0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(2 u^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(u - v^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(v^{2}\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 4 u,\\,1,\\,0\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(2 u^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(u - v^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(v^{2}\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 0,\\,- 2 v,\\,2 v\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v &=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & - 2 v & 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  &=\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ - 2 v & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}4 u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4 u & 1 \\\\ 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 2 v,\\,- 8 u v,\\,- 8 u v\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\| &=\\left\\|\\langle 2 v,\\,- 8 u v,\\,- 8 u v\\rangle\\right\\| \\\\  &=\\sqrt{\\left(2 v\\right)^2+\\left(- 8 u v\\right)^2+\\left(- 8 u v\\right)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{4 v^{2}+64 u^{2} v^{2}+64 u^{2} v^{2}} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{128 u^{2} v^{2} + 4 v^{2}} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{4 v^{2} \\left(32 u^{2} + 1\\right)} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{4 v^{2}}\\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1} \\\\  &=2 v \\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{v^2}=v$ since $v\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S &=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  &=\\iint_D 2 v \\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00cee569-5cea-5d57-9bc3-3903a71989af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558973} {:answer/id #uuid "0f83c76d-ec99-5241-aebf-9c4270e1a7ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u \\sqrt{v^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558976} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce6fda6-b951-5d87-8a85-6283f433ac85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558982} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1295a6-0f95-51f3-8fd1-ee96277ead12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558990} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a1a18a-411a-5224-a056-02a4076cbe0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558991} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ffdcbfe-e3c8-554c-90ac-7c7fa5fdd703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f12a3689-dba6-56d9-843d-c0f7bf0ac72a" :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\quad D=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=\\langle u + v,\\,3 - v,\\,5 u + 7 v + 1\\rangle$ for $0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(5 u + 7 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,5\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(5 u + 7 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,-1,\\,7\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v &=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  &=\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 5,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\| &=\\left\\|\\langle 5,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\right\\| \\\\  &=\\sqrt{\\left(5\\right)^2+\\left(-2\\right)^2+\\left(-1\\right)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{25+4+1} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{30} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S &=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  &=\\iint_D \\sqrt{30}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c5ae1a-9e7a-5d8b-ab09-dd7199e67922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561753} {:answer/id #uuid "71c1cca6-df0f-5b2c-a21a-33d4064bd36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561867} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3cea84-cf2c-5de8-8b3e-ec044d0938a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561930} {:answer/id #uuid "9af8d20b-17c6-5858-b1f2-709c110bbb7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561967} {:answer/id #uuid "f454b9f0-465f-5b23-8ad6-a4f87f6b3abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562190}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561753} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aaf5f5a-9372-5c9a-b844-dc81456ba76a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ca0b310-c54f-553d-a3d3-7e3881bc581e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95215" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 9$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R))=3\\cdot 9=27.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07714214-c4d9-5b08-a2c8-6c1f7e8ec203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561605} {:answer/id #uuid "1eea31bf-e3b1-5614-bfbd-d7f54109fa12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561665} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc27688-e270-59b6-bbc0-9254bca9da7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561937} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e18e7f-729e-5ce5-b8db-386712a47470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562044} {:answer/id #uuid "eccebf2d-98a8-5ea0-a876-f56acb406b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562175} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd01ea1f-a466-5fd9-8311-a2132252bc1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "43503f70-a039-563c-bedc-d1b2782aedca" :question/math-academy-id "q-106884" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 9$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R))=4\\cdot 9=36.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0401e5-9d3a-57a5-b046-48a14ed96f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562164} :answer-field/id #uuid "250a0410-56cf-50a6-9de1-7caaa7d5a608" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562244} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa32fab1-7f40-54da-a1e0-f1997cc223c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562007} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a725e18-61fa-5fa6-82d0-ff96754212ab" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562313} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9841f45c-6a19-51eb-88e2-92efb70ca08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561957} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc31d784-bb6f-5691-9bf1-b7d09bc77e17" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562342} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8b8037b-0482-5919-b25b-6c561eb8d3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562050} :answer-field/id #uuid "c78abd9f-d0b3-531a-96cd-b8ea8b5d6311" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d08973-12e3-5a13-bcc4-7e7a8399498d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561912} :answer-field/id #uuid "d18814a4-c3b7-50c0-86f8-c7a6e058383a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59f14881-731e-52d2-9146-dab6e581e8a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-253868" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{2}(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(2)\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{2}(2),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(2),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ \\since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02aca304-7b48-53d3-b89a-9ec1254a925a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561589} {:answer/id #uuid "77982fef-cbd9-5b64-9beb-aef93c81e8b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561878} {:answer/id #uuid "bb24ab63-f2c2-5312-9f4a-1f89c660eb2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562063} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cf2b8d-50b4-5dea-8cdb-f6d96e285ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562085} {:answer/id #uuid "e9436cfd-df38-58b3-bdb8-5acac9079d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561878} :answer-field/id #uuid "167770ed-e7de-54b6-9bfa-73c5e3df3786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7b8162a3-2a18-5498-a3a6-ab4fdf90d980" :question/math-academy-id "q-105158" :question/problem "Find $x$, if $5a-2x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-2x & =b \\\\ -2x & =b-5a \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}(b-5a) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "645b2e7b-ed0a-5176-a522-b400791972fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561828} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d7e4db-ae01-5275-b4c8-0fd8386be685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562002} {:answer/id #uuid "b27b7ca7-3a76-56c0-845b-41bdb8aea4a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562028} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf83f83-0973-5661-b724-fbd23032d35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562134} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a757e4-19f6-50f1-9887-1b2bae99eff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562185} :answer-field/id #uuid "a69d9c07-c486-5d96-be8f-04bbd335d396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7efa12c8-5625-54e7-8464-2de1d85a3ba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95218" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{7}(R),$ the vector space of all $7\\times 7$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{7}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{7}(R))={7}^{2}=49.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48724e40-2537-5993-a9ac-f90136d4706d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561765} {:answer/id #uuid "704ca1d5-8fbc-545a-a7fc-7fef0adaebcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561860} {:answer/id #uuid "ac081ada-e903-5cd6-86dc-983aac36c0ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562010} {:answer/id #uuid "e7eef5a1-6369-528c-8470-5e884104b19a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562153} {:answer/id #uuid "f989d214-29fe-5513-93bb-4bb8188098f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562199}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562153} :answer-field/id #uuid "fee18a9e-3a2d-5f93-9c78-89a367b51148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ec0dc53-4341-5ee6-84a6-d12231a31e63" :question/math-academy-id "q-106875" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R),$ the vector space of all $5\\times 1$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nThus, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R)$ contains $5\\cdot 1=5$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R),$ we have, indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R))=5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6bd69d-eb28-52f2-9e31-c5b78c1f5326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570980} {:answer/id #uuid "7df978ee-1cba-5e6d-a0df-7a5d2c0faa72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571088} {:answer/id #uuid "b58b8460-eabb-5831-9222-ae5190c87747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571171} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b666c4-2e1e-5e88-95b7-208ffe3d3b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571247} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9e80fa-761b-575b-8358-1633af610880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571088} :answer-field/id #uuid "2984758c-d2d0-5408-9558-da3f2d0bbdb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f805a2f-454d-5b6b-b94e-f4a78a778d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-131099" :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -25\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & ={t}_{11}{C}_{11}+{t}_{12}{C}_{12}+{t}_{13}{C}_{13}+{t}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}-10\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =-10{C}_{14} \\\\  & =-10\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & -10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & ∗\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10(-1)^{1+4}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =10(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =10(0-4(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)+0) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-40\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c409eaf-a88b-52d0-93e4-e06a7fbd8275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570931} {:answer/id #uuid "460aef30-08cb-5a20-8328-3ae1a8f946cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571000} {:answer/id #uuid "929a7047-4cab-559a-b902-fb43e8ec1d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571119} {:answer/id #uuid "e1597bd3-5f9c-5dd4-9a8d-382a63c04f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571237} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b70f72-82d4-57c3-b86c-4d54f9b46376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571000} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe34648-d2b0-538a-b862-47f017b7fd97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d888b8b4-33e2-5465-96a7-f2cf43e527f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-32746" :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(W) & ={w}_{11}{C}_{11}+{w}_{12}{C}_{12}+{w}_{13}{C}_{13}+{w}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+5\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & ={C}_{11}+5{C}_{13} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 5 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & 0 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & ∗ & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+5(2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =10(3\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-60\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a2628d9-2ef7-52ba-ae71-6150e6f2287d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572928} :answer-field/id #uuid "68889a3d-e5ee-5d54-9a14-6cd62d10349e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573184} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd34578d-2535-5f34-83c6-c3e44442de3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573147} :answer-field/id #uuid "91d3976d-329b-5f5a-af1a-743745fa1745" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5042d60d-1d74-5a97-ae4c-6f45b8b23e98" :question/math-academy-id "q-318151" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}).$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c})=\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}) & =2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+(-3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56b0dd0c-9d63-5a70-95a6-dd376b0a0954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572926} :answer-field/id #uuid "b37d9201-7dcf-5a89-a52b-32dccf01ca00" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573214} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83567ae1-8427-57f7-8486-981e82916dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572994} :answer-field/id #uuid "beab0d67-6115-58a0-9692-4c292e4251e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "df07f0e4-b8c0-55c8-a0ad-afd25f375b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-318154" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\hspace{0.167em}4\\mathbf{a}-(3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c}).$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$4\\mathbf{a}-(3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c})=\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\mathbf{a}-(3\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c}) & =4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-(3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-28 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4db8f3-59c2-52bd-bbc6-7b8fc5889531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574973} {:answer/id #uuid "423e1384-7818-5410-95a8-d0d39c518bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575063} {:answer/id #uuid "6c462f0d-0366-5361-9779-d3c8686baa44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575145} {:answer/id #uuid "a71763c5-31bc-5501-80a5-4ad95f6b3b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575242} {:answer/id #uuid "f4920f18-c1a5-58fd-9b3e-acfe48313d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575379}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575242} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c1e3e7-b7eb-5780-ac9f-e4101be00453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63775110-b76b-5425-a979-1d472435c3ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-107114" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $A=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b,c\\in R\\}.$    \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are two matrices from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is not a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is  *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an element of $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =k\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a & 2b \\\\ 2c & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is  *not* closed under scalar  multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $A$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5feb286-8b23-57c0-ac21-fed05b381fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581360} :answer-field/id #uuid "07597190-ac59-5428-a2ee-1bedde5eb782" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581508} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70b80b34-c5f9-50a3-ba76-9ee7ae35e9c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581218} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0bc86d1-6571-5da2-b952-6dc97f4ae7d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33cf76c5-c3a5-5b77-b26a-87104b2a8407" :question/math-academy-id "q-350410" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices above, write the following expression as a Hadamard product. Here $(\\,\\cdot \\,)$ denotes matrix multiplication.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$(AB)\\cdot (By)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25px\">$⊙$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n$-1$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n$4$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-2}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $u$ and $v$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\nv⊙u=\\text{diag}(v)u.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBy=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AB)\\cdot (By) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{diag}(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a302b5c4-0461-5009-8491-f7db4cd6ec71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581322} :answer-field/id #uuid "05c01a3c-37b3-5f5f-87ea-cd4fb3a3bcec" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581505} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e70a962-f692-51d3-a93b-f8ebbf0bddc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581276} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b79a7b-7f6b-543d-a8a3-27df8b85dc6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581525} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d4fa35-1579-5790-bbdc-96ca35065f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581353}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581353} :answer-field/id #uuid "a37b5d3e-3e0d-53e7-8116-4f3125d28473" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39b8d597-d04f-59be-b981-2d4d51fa5c59" :question/math-academy-id "q-350407" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices above, write the following expression as a Hadamard product. Here $(\\,\\cdot \\,)$ denotes matrix multiplication.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$(AB)\\cdot (By)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n$3$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25px\">$⊙$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n{{field-2}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-3}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $u$ and $v$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\nv⊙u=\\text{diag}(v)u.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBy=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AB)\\cdot (By) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{diag}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "523485e8-11e1-592f-85b0-00b08fc0a408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585323} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa3179c-4b19-5e60-91b5-26f9bf98f6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585355} {:answer/id #uuid "79c6ec69-d733-5d09-a84c-fce18f453c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585394} {:answer/id #uuid "c91b3930-4b4b-571f-9715-6dd69beb7bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585518} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a72a55-761b-54bc-b1b6-24849696e7f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585572}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585355} :answer-field/id #uuid "434c9117-8247-5029-ac1d-1d3a605e81e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "451265f5-e2bb-5604-8977-cb1160820683" :question/math-academy-id "q-106846" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d611de-4cf1-5bac-8543-03bc6cd035a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591821} {:answer/id #uuid "1613713d-3d9d-580a-9af1-50efd6d73a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591822} {:answer/id #uuid "17f92c96-54e0-5fd8-a6da-49db6161509d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591826} {:answer/id #uuid "44e1af84-83ce-5d10-b965-19718d8b8cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591884} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac09189-146b-5988-bf68-ba01646a487b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591884} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb94533f-d5c1-5e8b-82d2-efb3fb33faf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "51661d74-6629-571a-8a09-af0a0ec03e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-70439" :question/problem "Let $p=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $q=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $r=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $3p-(q+2r).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $3p-(q+2r),$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-(q+2r) & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e009ed-e38f-5f3f-b506-88fb6f0c9a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594367} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8c2ecb-71fd-5d9d-a8fe-54672a42986c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594380} {:answer/id #uuid "60b4d3f0-01fb-5268-a09e-429775a1fc04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594422} {:answer/id #uuid "80552fbd-6f46-5dcf-88a8-c56e7d8de874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594455} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf6ccc5-5d21-58fb-8af0-8f752aebe818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594380} :answer-field/id #uuid "3600e22d-0d76-5c25-b1ba-f39e7596b6f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "39d5931f-416f-5897-adc4-9cd73edc3ddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-40661" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={14}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d2f89bc-4263-53b3-a909-37a5a882978d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication by a scalar is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594417} {:answer/id #uuid "77bdd868-7eca-50b2-8540-a32a46a4fa26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of matrices in $Z$ is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594447} {:answer/id #uuid "999b7aa8-d034-5e5e-8e2f-f39250364dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ does not contain the zero matrix $O$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594480} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cf0f47-f015-5e95-a44a-63c22bfe741d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "there exists a matrix $A\\in Z$ such that $A+B\\ne O$ for any $B\\in Z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594563} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b0c5f8-8d89-5a74-a4a9-502ea0b1df4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594447} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6e18283-458f-52ba-b0db-921afa180c14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64ce04e1-24f2-5731-a9fd-09168e6ae104" :question/math-academy-id "q-106814" :question/problem "Let $Z$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $0.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Z$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of a matrix by a real number) because $\\text{____}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n*$Z$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of a matrix by a real number) because **addition of matrices in $Z$ is not closed.*** \n\nFor example, consider the matrices\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nin $Z.$ Then,\n\n$$\ndet(A+B)=det(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHence, $A+B\\notin Z.$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- The zero matrix belongs to $Z.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in Z,$ the additive inverse matrix $-A=(-1)\\cdot A$ is also in $Z$ since $det(-A)=(-1)^{2}\\cdot det(A)=1\\cdot 0=0,$ and $A+(-A)=O.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in Z,$ multiplication by a scalar $\\alpha \\in R$ is closed since $det(\\alpha A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot det(A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot 0=0.$ Hence, $\\alpha A\\in Z.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Multiplication of matrices with real entries by a scalar is associative, in general.    $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c6de01b-679a-5baa-9a9d-81da8f26e179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594361} {:answer/id #uuid "1f98e5d8-5a5c-5242-a16b-f58df045d0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594364} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5b24fe-9f45-5e26-ace1-4fff3693fa5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594468} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c0c59a-28f9-535b-8397-486d864cfb1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594486} {:answer/id #uuid "dee383af-ca45-5d80-9267-bca2474ee066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594468} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fb0e942-8154-5842-9cd7-b9f0e6c530a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8eb2b866-11c6-531d-8934-8f7819ddc747" :question/math-academy-id "q-124275" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=5u+3v+1,\\,y=3u+2v-2.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-3y-8,\\,v=by+cx+13,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u+3v+1 \\\\ 3u+2v-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u+3v \\\\ 3u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (x-1)+(-3)\\cdot (y+2) \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (x-1)+5\\cdot (y+2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x-3y-8 \\\\ 5y-3x+13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-3y-8,\\,v=5y+(-3)x+13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+5+(-3)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8b4298-844c-564c-9fab-4681da466ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594343} {:answer/id #uuid "30c39fc7-cff2-5154-9ca7-5086eb270961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594386} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5771f8-790c-5446-bfed-af77c825cee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594418} {:answer/id #uuid "8224e886-7c4c-54cb-a127-70dc78ac962b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594457} {:answer/id #uuid "eb890903-6220-58b5-87af-e7a9a4935482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594567}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594386} :answer-field/id #uuid "d591aacf-2752-506a-9108-d2b38a8989d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e6353c7d-5c28-59f2-8ec7-3961a7f70bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124222" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 6 \\\\ -4 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-5)-6\\cdot (-4)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+5+8=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15dcc281-2954-553e-85df-b2f0a146b6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596115} {:answer/id #uuid "5b63239f-68b4-56e6-908b-40798eb8c95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596225} {:answer/id #uuid "67c91ea2-823f-55f4-ad73-17c18ecb13e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596249} {:answer/id #uuid "872cf26f-b537-5f5a-b6b4-14bd0df480ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596296} {:answer/id #uuid "89c446c9-afb0-567e-8891-168dda7e77ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596249} :answer-field/id #uuid "6de0975b-c189-5b10-aee7-198b7786f2cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "063aeb47-cc32-5866-89d5-e236fc5f0c86" :question/math-academy-id "q-74980" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ ${a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -5 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e43282c-0cf0-5b3e-8a8a-ad676b6134c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596100} {:answer/id #uuid "14511788-1583-59d2-b77f-e7cd0e3d763c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596111} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0978f6-2a63-5dec-9711-3250e7457b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596119} {:answer/id #uuid "22b42229-2e2d-5606-bf7e-90c340abf4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596133} {:answer/id #uuid "6c20b417-687c-5436-a128-a05c0b007a9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596256} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd2731ad-3315-5e8a-9ee3-66123d65a523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "08036111-0c20-583b-9b87-91af757f0cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-110757" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=-3\\cdot {a}_{1}+0\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ff4cbf-d10b-587a-9313-21d50adb5ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596132} {:answer/id #uuid "49ca982d-0247-572e-a0f5-a3887bcc99bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596199} {:answer/id #uuid "5352babd-73c2-5353-8571-1b5fb9d81589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596212} {:answer/id #uuid "5950f61e-cce8-51e9-acb6-abafe84a2dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596221} {:answer/id #uuid "e8dd74f8-2931-5777-8cfb-fa0f5cb1787a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596451}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596212} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ea9610-1efe-5517-a4ae-7cc819f08b86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "38a7008b-1116-5fac-b1ec-aa79c81d6e02" :question/math-academy-id "q-110854" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}11 & 13 & 26 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "815dd135-2f41-5db2-b59c-f8dafeffa320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596289} {:answer/id #uuid "850e0fea-bfbd-59ce-99bc-1fcd13adebe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/879cc00fedd506a0607bce3d3d36f99b489f599dadb6b13d4d1176fa24441853.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596293} {:answer/id #uuid "d234dbf4-16a0-5807-84f8-8b26bb66d146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eb9741219f07c5e1d980a0a028e9ed15f7b2da2f85e7194a20f981a1b9cc670c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596411} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d244e7-a760-517a-b4ad-c9c55c2d6d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/0259e59ebaeac1ddae01ed390b048d070bd37d99610bcb7d8956d095b3747aa9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596428} {:answer/id #uuid "e124269c-4968-56b5-a8fd-f61c8f0472af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5e/5e822ce487f9ff6b4adbcf7a801a391b4ddf3f65d111a3c26f3a0c34fac8a45d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596442}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596442} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c50e3d6-fc0a-56d5-a77f-01ef04e202cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "402436bb-c30c-59bd-8a9d-f3ea4ce51b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-334628" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[2,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+2 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/47deaca4362c32ef92833c1aae0c533a05314caf4ad4ccd2b685c3b4bc7436dc.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+2 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+4 \\\\ y &=t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/55/55741e66507ccc28af5bc5e7e347bf899c5c4bf2cc3d1a687be3c6c9c0fb4e01.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2t-4 \\\\ y &=-t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/1b/1bc4e458b50d98fba82a3220d35982d5b034c0d64a5977bd2be81527b1b77f00.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5e/5e822ce487f9ff6b4adbcf7a801a391b4ddf3f65d111a3c26f3a0c34fac8a45d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27256e8f-d3cf-59fb-8060-f42b95f9deab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596142} {:answer/id #uuid "29be70c2-9294-5715-9438-7c1153e924d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596146} {:answer/id #uuid "472a4b3c-e006-5386-844f-0ef2340eed4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596192} {:answer/id #uuid "6b832298-c453-5422-8bd4-7cf085e2ad4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596254} {:answer/id #uuid "b4135ea3-d99d-5a9d-8b47-a7b5f836b99f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596146} :answer-field/id #uuid "080aaf41-1f60-5cca-9230-579fa1e8b02f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b982763-01fc-513f-86c1-2bf91c0724be" :question/math-academy-id "q-110750" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a761d71-0562-59a6-ae91-872bf370da31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596224} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3c2351-6a2e-5dec-b72c-8412b6a16dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596253} {:answer/id #uuid "7d307d12-7fdf-53ff-8d68-10bf29b50800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596280} {:answer/id #uuid "d00c3747-bb88-59fa-94b5-ca65232de916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596405} {:answer/id #uuid "d05522fd-eacc-55a5-9983-016429074252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596253} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e892215-29b1-513c-8eb3-bece5cb2c5be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "77074f06-1b98-5a52-b190-4d83a56a5953" :question/math-academy-id "q-110752" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{1}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1\\cdot {a}_{2}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{1}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b07dd60-0014-580b-8b23-78839be25852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597173} {:answer/id #uuid "a214cd1c-072c-5a2d-b5b9-ec3aac007615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597187} {:answer/id #uuid "babf80de-ab7b-5fa6-afcb-667db2096c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597234} {:answer/id #uuid "bc36b370-54f3-5c7d-8245-8bf486748860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597238} {:answer/id #uuid "ec496296-8a8a-5ebe-aff5-583859ef562a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597336} :answer-field/id #uuid "e64f5172-0e1c-5f64-9dab-127dd5f4ac4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "00e47a8b-bfdb-57ef-bc67-0afa88aed787" :question/math-academy-id "q-34813" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\})=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5dc50cf-a44a-5e44-bc12-fef4935ac845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597262} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3a84df-12db-55df-9c89-82bffcfdded2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597268} {:answer/id #uuid "e52e14b3-c1f2-51b2-a2e5-060ffc384875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597325} {:answer/id #uuid "f39e9d6b-3fa9-5018-afb5-8fca4db9fe93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597356} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ee687f-1581-5e64-a53b-815a4ce4e1e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597370}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597356} :answer-field/id #uuid "3797cfe1-4b24-55db-baa7-084d15b6d8b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c99018e-042c-5e6e-a45e-3d5b191e8812" :question/math-academy-id "q-79141" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 5 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb3b421-80f4-59bc-bcbe-925e19f466ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596889} {:answer/id #uuid "5c39bedd-ebc4-5c1f-8910-930c884394ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597053} {:answer/id #uuid "b71ca972-0565-5e82-b6dc-614a675e50f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597226} {:answer/id #uuid "bc28736a-29df-5974-9c16-d63beebd954d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597237} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ffeab0-4d50-53b3-9776-a10ce606b7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596889} :answer-field/id #uuid "eba676eb-61a7-53d5-a921-5b1590c611f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e030566-38fa-5b41-b40b-6a796032582d" :question/math-academy-id "q-34852" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 16 & -12 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 16 & -12 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183f517f-71f7-5626-b801-9643afed044f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596909} {:answer/id #uuid "2027e14d-67b1-58c2-a33f-dbb8717e83e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596929} {:answer/id #uuid "37aee036-10a0-546a-81fa-35399ea9e90a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596976} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3f2353-f200-5d6f-a0d2-fe17bdaad0ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596981} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9f7e8f-d50b-52e6-9e48-16bbff5d1f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597139}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596981} :answer-field/id #uuid "595567f9-3391-5304-a79f-98411a455d55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8552671-09f8-5f0e-a8a6-71b4905e8538" :question/math-academy-id "q-35803" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ and $C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 3 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49334653-e38c-5cfb-9873-fb7e6355a4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597936} {:answer/id #uuid "61f263e0-e23b-5001-bf35-66bd8dc94667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598006} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2826dd-f6d5-5153-92ad-01eed8379669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598027} {:answer/id #uuid "b9eee763-9216-5884-8736-047fe1506dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598229} {:answer/id #uuid "dea99316-c068-5191-b371-03ee0ef8d677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598027} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1294a6b-7a5b-5151-862a-e0226394cede" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07c9b439-0b1a-5e05-8013-bc2d8c8362e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80582" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$ \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$  \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$ \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =4\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)=8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 3+(-5)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (-1)=-2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{2}$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60457e12-d1f0-59e0-8b86-add76f35cfc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598002} {:answer/id #uuid "6c089429-ecff-58c4-8e47-ba740e372da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598034} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe4aa69-4348-5b9d-973c-c78d3ac50a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598048} {:answer/id #uuid "b8568286-36ea-5b86-bb99-c3252c4f51bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598226} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2c1d92-a385-5928-9c77-79f72586157f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598380}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598034} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4e515d-4b86-5300-a5ee-85d812adeaa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15c5bdc4-266d-55b7-bc54-693f9e46ea72" :question/math-academy-id "q-113601" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{7}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{7}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2ef30b-0a76-51d2-b85b-f24f2ab94f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597905} {:answer/id #uuid "4923f7bc-8197-54ee-befa-ee0f4c242b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597935} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3b8862-16fb-537b-ad3a-266670a9672c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598028} {:answer/id #uuid "78d25f24-068c-5525-9d5b-ec99b0d5cd17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598073} {:answer/id #uuid "b3379fee-e7d1-5cb1-9c6b-7880162ee488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598216} :answer-field/id #uuid "39eff8a0-f8b0-5904-9d84-e115306efef5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "392438bc-ef52-54c2-85fc-68f435a450cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-39632" :question/problem "Find ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1} &=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -6 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a17a7f-c7a7-5c40-b228-0b3b4550812c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597767} {:answer/id #uuid "46b4e1ee-0ae9-5661-aa00-742fed89e052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597927} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6ddcf9-8121-5948-a8ee-050544584f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598131} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6fbb33-d432-527f-95fa-647f9987ee8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598233} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d871fc-7604-5730-a8e0-a80e6d37a1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598131} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9f7fa1-c5a6-5d6a-ae66-a5c6dbaebcab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "39c05cc7-c6fd-59d5-885c-b53a7252f425" :question/math-academy-id "q-80580" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let  $h=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ A vector  $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to $h.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot h$ for $i=1,2,3.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot h & =2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 5=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot h & =(-1)\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 5=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot h & =(-1)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 5=-31\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa8ff41-4bae-52b2-8cf2-59d78774d8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597866} {:answer/id #uuid "582f0e99-8e50-5040-ab83-e1981c6d5145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597979} {:answer/id #uuid "61d277b1-a3ba-5de5-86a7-b6af597f4451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598005} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6f0b3e-7048-5781-87b3-eddeaa2b815d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598121} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cd8b7d-dc0e-5880-98d0-878addee64dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598121} :answer-field/id #uuid "9652e9a8-4102-5d95-9147-7a92f15547c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6749e6b6-a72d-5aac-abff-73843413b89e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80512" :question/problem "For the vector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $$\n\\displaystyle W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2},{h}_{3}\\},\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}\\}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{3} & =1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $V.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)=1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $W.$ Notice that, again,  we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287c503e-7c8a-5f0c-9c6b-0b5b9cdd8674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597848} {:answer/id #uuid "38fb66a8-d5ad-5eb1-87ff-20e679e2d9d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597895} {:answer/id #uuid "56fcfe5c-609d-582e-a0ec-49210f06434f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597977} {:answer/id #uuid "8debd5b3-7379-508a-ae74-67baf12da9bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598125} {:answer/id #uuid "f490152d-375d-528a-98ba-a95fcbd77ced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597977} :answer-field/id #uuid "735179c2-14ff-5b22-90a7-3d20078776b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d6e4ea2-827c-5957-a029-9ac064268cf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-39917" :question/problem "For the vector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $$\n\\displaystyle V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $V,H$ and $W$ as $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =2\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 5=19\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is not orthogonal to the set $V.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $H:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot (-1)=0,\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-1)=-1\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, $u$ is not orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot (-6)+2\\cdot 1=0\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-8)+2\\cdot (-5)=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02302f24-45e4-5d33-8c48-0d5b5736ab65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597759} {:answer/id #uuid "ae658add-eba8-55ae-a81e-faca0857a35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598206} {:answer/id #uuid "c581186d-1b07-54dd-8635-cbd003160e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598260} {:answer/id #uuid "d37734f4-e95c-56f6-a418-1aa397321b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598292} {:answer/id #uuid "ed54c90c-e22a-51e7-be37-5aa9a9afffa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597759} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd869db4-71a7-5b1a-8801-9d8d809159b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7d7e6ab1-6e1a-5af8-a8d8-cd18338dc919" :question/math-academy-id "q-41673" :question/problem "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}).$\n So, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}) \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce6b3ea-037c-573b-abbe-4bcbb939156e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597862} {:answer/id #uuid "5cee1f37-1759-5b82-af11-404a084e43d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597994} {:answer/id #uuid "80112803-1c35-5810-ad08-cabbd8bdc61f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598089} {:answer/id #uuid "947ec4ac-8027-5bfa-96de-035dfeee873d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598150} {:answer/id #uuid "cd62c13d-ce00-598e-92df-558b279ec151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598150} :answer-field/id #uuid "89cd61fb-3e56-5cbd-b576-8c8e7aaa1d99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8332fce6-98e9-5e59-9cd9-6ba1d1f19a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-82605" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 8-5\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2198c611-7173-5cbb-bc05-98bdc9fc8599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597833} {:answer/id #uuid "3c5e8238-c71b-5b5b-9e07-33e0f4bc5487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597901} {:answer/id #uuid "685744bc-2c99-5cd5-b48e-b0f56db78a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598024} {:answer/id #uuid "73e346b3-296a-5774-bc03-f0f117bb02ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598061} {:answer/id #uuid "8ceb7c3e-cd29-5ae4-b82c-a54bfa1b5aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598123}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597833} :answer-field/id #uuid "521d04e0-7336-56dd-9712-07a65580ed82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "948a85e9-1e04-5c01-b5ae-ee755fcf61c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140463" :question/problem "For the vector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $$\n\\displaystyle V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\},\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ and $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =4\\cdot (-5)+0\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot (-4)=-40\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =4\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =4\\cdot (-5)+0\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 4=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =4\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 1=5\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{3} & =4\\cdot 5+0\\cdot 0+5\\cdot (-4)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =4\\cdot (-5)+0\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 4=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =4\\cdot 0+0\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1500192f-1f7d-5f27-957a-3b76fe7e1fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597803} {:answer/id #uuid "316207e2-7884-55aa-9c8e-087c864d6a25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597869} {:answer/id #uuid "620b07aa-3ef7-5199-ad9c-a68245604dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598007} {:answer/id #uuid "897ee41a-2f38-5b0c-bee0-365e27e60617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598115} {:answer/id #uuid "c8499429-b52b-5464-9dab-fe4afe013ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186597803} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe75c2f0-dbc1-5e69-8302-e639d52be51b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d8c2fe8-a90e-5d60-9d0e-8396a7a4a181" :question/math-academy-id "q-140465" :question/problem "Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},$ find the value of $\\frac{a-b}{c}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{5}{3} & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{16}{3}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{16}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3$, we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-16,$ $b=5,$ and $c=3.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a-b}{c}=\\frac{-16-5}{3}=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c47173-b2f7-50e6-819d-f00441ae971a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597774} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb367e5-a781-5d25-9b1e-8b36e2941190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597814} {:answer/id #uuid "86bd8528-6749-55ce-a075-edc79c53aeb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598105} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4e3225-68f6-57ab-831b-7650bde1b69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598135} {:answer/id #uuid "f24e8cfa-51dc-5001-9c8e-81cad56fb4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598359} :answer-field/id #uuid "028d60a3-6607-58f1-8329-5830b9ff8566" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a08a3a69-98a5-5b6f-976d-a369abf8bcbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-39949" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$    \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot (-4)=0 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-4)\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-4)\\cdot 3+5\\cdot 3+3\\cdot (-4)=-9\\ne 0 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-7)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 2=5\\ne 0 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  \"${v}_{1}$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0040754a-5789-5a7f-94fa-48a30119d889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597753} {:answer/id #uuid "645f47ae-8cd8-53fc-8a78-6f6a362c7472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598013} {:answer/id #uuid "77501d55-b8a1-5a31-a2c1-e6cabc512675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598072} {:answer/id #uuid "96d95cf4-53b5-5706-b88a-4a704e112b7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598158} {:answer/id #uuid "fa997327-756e-54b4-b408-44c5d96af80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598383}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598072} :answer-field/id #uuid "63805426-d2b9-5fd4-aa34-e65e42424111" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb6180b1-8d7c-5e70-b913-270aaafaaaaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-82711" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5971a48c-dc96-5290-8c0a-f328fe12dfbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186597983} {:answer/id #uuid "757fcb47-7343-552c-b1e4-6259bda02262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598067} {:answer/id #uuid "88fe0e74-ae2b-577c-8bb7-c536c725c1b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598111} {:answer/id #uuid "90cdc7aa-5fc6-5e16-a8b3-cec2bf3a73e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598138} {:answer/id #uuid "a55376a1-0da9-554b-94cd-190f664d49db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598067} :answer-field/id #uuid "99cbea4f-10a2-5e3a-b679-4e7162882ea8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9abaad0-0e9e-51c8-a5f9-be0eaadf6c41" :question/math-academy-id "q-80527" :question/problem "Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{5+1}{-2}=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20829bea-c21f-573c-8557-b35c5bf2ea5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598762} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ff9bdf-d2f6-5767-8572-267f6aff28c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598971} {:answer/id #uuid "b8836cfe-bf53-5282-b629-bf84eba48ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599005} {:answer/id #uuid "be45abbb-73a6-5d49-82ce-763211609f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599010} {:answer/id #uuid "cba7a2c0-1176-5c86-b2a5-695dad4bd1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599032}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599005} :answer-field/id #uuid "725729df-e452-5e37-9cca-e06d8c168486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "32eff1fe-7494-54b9-8784-b8b3a9fa21e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-140487" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{3}}?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{4} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{21}{5} & \\frac{6}{5} & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &=-\\frac{5}{21}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{2}{7} & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{7}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{2}{7}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{7}{x}_{4}+{x}_{4} &=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{6}{7}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{7}{x}_{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}{x}_{4} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=7,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}]}
